{
  "why": [
    "The section 2.6 Reading and ProbeManifest vocabulary, read by both",
    "implementations: cli/internal/model (which builds readings in the probe",
    "runner) and shared/culprit_model/readings.py (which validates what the",
    "API is asked to store). A verdict one side can emit and the other",
    "refuses is a reading that vanishes between the probe and the database.",
    "",
    "Written before the Go side exists, deliberately, for the same reason",
    "subject_conformance.json was: two implementations reconciled after the",
    "fact agree on whatever they both happened to do, not on what was",
    "specified. Add a case here, not to one language's test file.",
    "",
    "`rejected` cases must fail validation in BOTH languages. Each names the",
    "invariant it exists to hold, so a future reader can tell a deliberate",
    "refusal from an accident of parsing."
  ],
  "verdicts": [
    {"name": "pass", "needs_reason": false},
    {"name": "fail", "needs_reason": false},
    {"name": "inconclusive", "needs_reason": true},
    {"name": "refused", "needs_reason": true}
  ],
  "effects": [
    {"name": "inspect", "allowed": true},
    {"name": "observe", "allowed": true},
    {"name": "interrogate", "allowed": true},
    {"name": "mutate", "allowed": false}
  ],
  "costs": ["cheap", "metered", "intrusive"],
  "manifests_accepted": [
    {
